KW  - MLOps
KW  - Machine Learning Operations
KW  - DevOps
KW  - FinTech
KW  - Model Governance
KW  - Organisational Readiness
KW  - Enterprise AI
AB  - The rapid industrialisation of artificial intelligence has elevated Machine Learning Operations (MLOps) into a foundational discipline for organisations seeking to deploy, monitor, and sustain machine learning models in production. Despite a growing body of literature on technical pipeline orchestration and continuous delivery for ML, significant gaps persist in how enterprises-particularly within the financial services and FinTech sectors-integrate interpretability requirements, address organisational readiness, and govern data-driven decision-making at scale. This paper presents a systematic literature review (SLR) following the PRISMA protocol, synthesising findings from 170 peer-reviewed publications spanning 2015 to 2025. We examine three interconnected themes: (i) MLOps frameworks, maturity models, and pipeline orchestration practices; (ii) explainability and interpretability integration within MLOps lifecycles; and (iii) organisational, team-level, and governance factors that determine successful enterprise MLOps adoption. Drawing on the SPACE and DORA frameworks for developer productivity analogy, we further contextualise these findings within large-scale FinTech settings, highlighting the particular pressures of regulatory compliance (SR 11-7, GDPR, EU AI Act), model risk management, and real-time fraud detection pipelines. We propose a four-tier integrative MLOps Maturity and Interpretability Framework (MMIF) and identify eight priority research gaps requiring empirical investigation. Our findings offer actionable guidance for practitioners, engineering leads, and product managers responsible for operationalising AI within enterprise environments.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
T1  - MLOps in freight rail operations
AU  - Pineda-Jaramillo, Juan
AU  - Viti, Francesco
JO  - Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence
VL  - 123
SP  - 106222
PY  - 2023
DA  - 2023/08/01/
SN  - 0952-1976
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engappai.2023.106222
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0952197623004062
KW  - Freight rail operations
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - MLOps
KW  - CI/CD/CT
KW  - Apache airflow
KW  - ML deployment
AB  - Railways are essential for freight transport due to their operational reliability advantages, but maintaining this advantage requires optimised railway infrastructure. Previous research has developed models to predict freight rail disruptions/disturbances and their associated delay times, in order to better understand the impact of multiple factors on them. However, because these models are built on static datasets, extracting real value from a model in a production environment remains difficult. This paper presents a methodology that demonstrates the potential of MLOps in automating the entire workflow, from data extraction to model deployment for real-time delay predictions in freight rail operations, including good practices of Continuous-Integration, Continuous-Delivery, and Continuous-Training, as well as a tool list for each process. Our research advances the field of railway operations by developing an entire MLOps workflow using data from the freight rail operations of the Luxembourgish National Freight Railway Company over a seventeen-month period. Furthermore, we employed a LightGBM model that had previously performed well in another study. This workflow can be automatically triggered to develop the processes and thus maintain an ML model capable of predicting delay times for CFL Multimodal operations in real-time. Our findings demonstrate that MLOps have the potential to automate the entire process, opening up new avenues for future research in this field. Although the methodology presented is intended to optimise freight rail operations for a specific company, it can be easily transferable to other railway companies or other transportation industries, such as aviation, shipping, and trucking.
ER  - 

